# ABP Studio MCP Tools - AI Usage Guide

ABP Studio exposes 14 MCP tools through the `abp mcp-studio` bridge. They let you monitor the running applications, drive the Solution Runner and explore the solution structure.

ABP Studio must be running. The monitoring tools work on their own, the build and solution tools need an open solution, and the application, container and task tools also need a selected run profile.

## Tool Categories at a Glance

| Category | Purpose | Tools |
|---|---|---|
| Monitoring | Observe running apps (logs, errors, requests, events) | `get_logs`, `get_exceptions`, `get_requests`, `get_events` |
| Applications | Start and stop applications | `start_applications`, `stop_applications` |
| Containers | Start and stop Docker containers | `start_containers`, `stop_containers` |
| Build | Compile and prepare the solution | `dotnet_build`, `install_libs` |
| Tasks | Run a configured task | `run_task` |
| Solution Structure | Explore the solution, modules and packages | `get_solution_info`, `list_modules`, `list_packages` |

---

## Workflows

### 1. Understanding a Solution (Start Here)

Always begin with discovery before taking action:

```
get_solution_info          -- solution name, path, template, module count, selected run profile
  -> list_modules          -- see all modules
  -> list_packages         -- see packages/projects, optionally filtered by module
```

### 2. Running Applications

```
start_applications         -- apps: ["<application-name>"], or folder: "Backend/API", or root: true
stop_applications          -- same targeting options
```

`start_applications` also restarts: an application that is already running is stopped and started again, so use it after a code change too.

Do not call the monitoring tools right after starting an application. Use them when the user reports a problem or asks what is happening.

### 3. Infrastructure (Docker Containers)

```
start_containers           -- containers: ["<container-name>"], or omit to start all
stop_containers            -- containers: [...], or omit to stop all
```

Containers are typically infrastructure dependencies (databases, Redis, RabbitMQ). Start them before starting applications.

### 4. Building

```
dotnet_build               -- root: true, or modules: [...], or packages: [...]
install_libs               -- when wwwroot/libs is missing client-side packages
```

### 5. Running Tasks

Tasks are one-shot operations (database migrations, seed data, npm install, etc.):

```
run_task                   -- task: "Migrate Database", timeout_seconds: 300
```

Task names come from the run profile and can contain spaces. They are matched in full, ignoring case only.

